WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.729 --> 00:00:05.310
Crisis on Infinite Earths has given Arrowverse
fans their first big crossover moment between

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:10.690
the DC film and TV universes with a surprising
run-in between movie Flash Ezra Miller and

00:00:10.690 --> 00:00:12.589
TV Flash Grant Gustin.

00:00:12.589 --> 00:00:15.799
But there's more to the shocking cameo than
meets the eye.

00:00:15.799 --> 00:00:20.470
Crisis on Infinite Earths executive producer
Marc Guggenheim opened up to Entertainment

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:25.550
Weekly, revealing just how involved DC Comics
chief content officer Jim Lee actually was

00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:30.189
in pulling off one of the TV season's most
exciting, and unexpected, appearances, saying:

00:00:30.189 --> 00:00:33.600
"Basically, I was really working hand-in-hand
with Jim.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:37.320
From the moment we found out that this was
a possibility, to once we knew Grant Gustin

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:41.480
was cool and on board with it and Ezra was
on board with it, I would send drafts of the

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:46.500
scene to both Jim Lee and [DC Entertainment's
film VP] Adam Schlagman, and we would just

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:47.940
go back and forth."

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:52.320
As Marvel and DC have expanded their screen
presences over the last decade, they've had

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:56.929
to contend with one glaring issue: how their
cinematic universes fit in with their television

00:00:56.929 --> 00:00:57.929
ones.

00:00:57.929 --> 00:01:01.809
It's been especially tough for DC, which has
had more luck among critics and fans with

00:01:01.809 --> 00:01:05.300
its animated and TV show properties than it
has with its movies.

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:10.510
Up until now, the CW line-up especially has
been key to DC's small screen dominance, in

00:01:10.510 --> 00:01:13.300
part because it hasn't tried to merge it with
the film division.

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:17.980
It's a narrative ecosystem in which writers
drop Easter eggs about one production into

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:22.390
another but usually do nothing beyond that,
which makes Miller's Crisis appearance the

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:27.550
most serious and significant attempt at a
DC cinematic-TV connection to date.

00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:31.720
It's also one Guggenheim and Lee were very
careful with executing, for the sake of every

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:32.720
universe.

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:33.720
Guggenheim told EW:

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:37.000
"The trick with the scene is...they didn't
want me to do anything that stepped on the

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:39.490
toes of the Flashpoint movie that they're
developing.

00:01:39.490 --> 00:01:41.170
So, we really worked hand-in-hand.

00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:44.920
Jim was really great about just making sure
that all the parties were talking to each

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:46.470
other, that nothing was getting stalled.

00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:51.030
He was a cheerleader when he needed to be,
a diplomat when he needed to be."

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:55.070
They may have taken a carefully planned approach
to writing the Barry-meets-Barry exchange,

00:01:55.070 --> 00:01:58.250
but how Miller's cameo came about was much
more impromptu.

00:01:58.250 --> 00:02:01.700
In a Variety interview, Guggenheim revealed
that Warner Bros.

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:05.420
Television president Peter Roth reached out
with a proposition after Arrow had wrapped,

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:07.650
and the crossover was nearly completed.

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:11.859
When Roth asked how Guggenheim would be able
to do it, the EP responded with:

00:02:11.859 --> 00:02:15.391
"'Yeah, I know, but if you're telling me Ezra
Miller can be in the crossover, I can make

00:02:15.391 --> 00:02:16.391
it happen.'"

00:02:16.391 --> 00:02:20.950
Guggenheim, Roth, and Lee delivered something
many fans thought was an improbable dream.

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:25.750
But it was one that writer, director, podcaster,
and all-around king of geeks Kevin Smith alluded

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:30.870
to the possibility of during a segment cut
from DC's Dawn Of The Justice League TV special.

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:36.310
During a 2016 episode of his Fat Man on Batman
podcast, Smith recounted his conversation

00:02:36.310 --> 00:02:39.829
with DC Creative Officer Geoff Johns, saying:

00:02:39.829 --> 00:02:43.540
"DC is known for nothing if not a multiverse.'"

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:44.540
"Right?"

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:47.080
"So at which point I was like, 'Could they
f--ing crossover?'

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:49.510
And he was like, 'Ahh.'"

00:02:49.510 --> 00:02:53.439
Miller may have also put out positive vibes
for a potential crossover while brushing off

00:02:53.439 --> 00:02:59.060
a bit of controversy around the DC film side's
decision to cast him instead of merely using

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:01.430
Gustin, who was already playing the speedster
on TV.

00:03:01.430 --> 00:03:06.281
In a July 2015 interview with MTV News, The
Flash star called the existence of two Barry

00:03:06.281 --> 00:03:07.609
Allens, quote, "awesome."

00:03:07.609 --> 00:03:08.939
"It's parallel universes!"

00:03:08.939 --> 00:03:10.269
"It's all good!"

00:03:10.269 --> 00:03:15.049
"Grant Gustin is the Flash, and I'm the Flash,
don't you see?

00:03:15.049 --> 00:03:16.969
It's the event horizon!"

00:03:16.969 --> 00:03:21.390
Casual fans will undoubtedly see Miller's
cameo as a "dope" one-off moment, but for

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:26.269
those a bit more up-to-speed on the comics
and various screen universes, the implications

00:03:26.269 --> 00:03:27.859
are way more exciting.

00:03:27.859 --> 00:03:32.049
To start, the run-in confirms the existence
of a multiverse through Miller's appearance,

00:03:32.049 --> 00:03:34.340
something that's corroborated by Gustin's
line:

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:35.730
"This should be impossible now."

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:37.090
"This should be impossible now?"

00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:41.219
Technically, it shouldn't be possible and
yet, there the two Barrys were, proving that

00:03:41.219 --> 00:03:45.829
DC may have finally settled on the multiverse
as the official answer to and connection between

00:03:45.829 --> 00:03:47.579
its big- and small-screen stories.

00:03:47.579 --> 00:03:51.219
But there are also hints about the version
of Barry Allen viewers can expect in Miller's

00:03:51.219 --> 00:03:53.349
upcoming Flash film.

00:03:53.349 --> 00:03:57.420
After being repeatedly delayed and going through
multiple creative team changes, The Flash

00:03:57.420 --> 00:03:59.920
movie is pretty difficult to get a read on.

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:04.159
Over the years that the film has been in development,
it's become less clear what exactly audiences

00:04:04.159 --> 00:04:08.639
are in for when the Andy Muschietti-directed
movie finally premieres in 2022.

00:04:08.639 --> 00:04:13.299
However, the Crisis on Infinite Earths cameo
did help decipher some of that.

00:04:13.299 --> 00:04:17.170
During their brief encounter, both Flashes
weighed the possibility of being face-to-face

00:04:17.170 --> 00:04:18.440
for different reasons.

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:22.950
As the cameo scene illustrates, Miller's Barry
was way less in the know than his counterpart.

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:26.970
His general confusion over where he is, as
well as how he arrived there, reveals that

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:31.040
he may be significantly less experienced than
Gustin's Barry is when it comes to his full

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:33.890
meta-human abilities, and his knowledge of
the multiverse.

00:04:33.890 --> 00:04:38.850
It's a perfect place for a film which will
reportedly be based on Andy Kubert and Geoff

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:40.970
Johns' Flashpoint novel to start.

00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:43.410
Miller's final line in Crisis on Infinite
Earths...

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:46.120
"I told Victor this was possible."

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:50.690
...also doubles as a nod to fellow Justice
League member Cyborg, and serve as confirmation

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:54.810
that Barry may bring his newfound knowledge
of the multiverse to his upcoming standalone

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:55.810
film.

00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:59.920
Additionally, the scene also seems to introduce
Miller's Allen to his superhero name, believe

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:01.350
it or not.

00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:03.220
"I'm also the Flash."

00:05:03.220 --> 00:05:05.420
"You're also the what?"

00:05:05.420 --> 00:05:10.500
That makes sense if the 2022 movie serves
as an origin tale for the DCEU speedster,

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:13.280
and not a story in which he's already a fully-fledged
hero.

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:17.260
With how well Miller's Barry seemingly takes
to the moniker, fans can maybe look forward

00:05:17.260 --> 00:05:21.370
to seeing a nod to the crossover moment from
the big-screen speedster in his forthcoming

00:05:21.370 --> 00:05:22.370
movie.

00:05:22.370 --> 00:05:26.100
While Ezra Miller's Crisis cameo was but a
few minutes long, it may have played a pivotal

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:30.810
role in connecting DC's universes, moving
the troubled franchise forward and launching

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:34.050
one of its best-known superheroes into a new
adventure.

